WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.902 line:0%
[ Siren wailing ]

00:00:02.969 --> 00:00:08.441 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:11.678 line:0%
-Cassie! Cassie,
I need you at the triage.

00:00:11.745 --> 00:00:12.878 line:0%
-Wait.

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:15.314 line:0%
O'Neil, will you let me drive?

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:16.715 line:0%
-What are you talking about?

00:00:16.782 --> 00:00:19.752 line:0%
-I ju-- I don't know,
I just have a weird feeling.

00:00:19.818 --> 00:00:22.488 line:0%
-Well, if you had
a bad experience on the job,

00:00:22.554 --> 00:00:24.257
don't let it mess
with your head.

00:00:25.224 --> 00:00:26.826
-Cassie, come on!

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:32.198
-Welcome back
to "Late Night," everybody.

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:34.133
We're here with Dakota Johnson.

00:00:34.199 --> 00:00:36.302
That was a clip
from "Madame Web."

00:00:36.369 --> 00:00:37.836
This is in the larger --
this takes place

00:00:37.903 --> 00:00:40.006
in the larger
Spider-Man universe.

00:00:40.073 --> 00:00:42.508
-It's actually not
the Spider-Man universe.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:43.977
-How would I say it?
The Spider-Verse?

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:47.346
-This is -- no, it is
a completely standalone world.

00:00:47.413 --> 00:00:48.715
It's Madame Web's world.

00:00:48.781 --> 00:00:50.383
-Oh, so I shouldn't use
the word "spider" at all?

00:00:50.449 --> 00:00:52.251
-No.
Well, there are spiders.

00:00:52.318 --> 00:00:54.019
It's like a little bit Spidey...

00:00:54.086 --> 00:00:55.554
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:55.621 --> 00:00:58.558
-...but it's not
in the Spider-verse.

00:00:58.624 --> 00:01:00.125
-Standalone.
-Standalone.

00:01:00.192 --> 00:01:01.394
Madame Web's world.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:03.563
-Don't need to worry
about having any

00:01:03.629 --> 00:01:05.531
predetermined ideas
or information about --

00:01:05.598 --> 00:01:08.000
-No. You don't have to know
anything about anything --

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.335
[ Laughter ]

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:12.705
...at all to watch this movie.

00:01:12.772 --> 00:01:15.241
-Should I know, like, what
ambulances are? 'Cause, like...

00:01:15.308 --> 00:01:16.342
-No.
-Okay.

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.044
-You will learn.
-You could learn...

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:19.779
Wow, so this is really, like,
an educational...

00:01:19.846 --> 00:01:22.415
-You don't got to know nothing.
It's great for America.

00:01:22.482 --> 00:01:24.316
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:26.886
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.155
-It'd be really --
that'd be the really --

00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:30.456
the funniest thing
to put on a poster.

00:01:30.523 --> 00:01:33.292
Like, "You don't got
to know nothing."

00:01:33.359 --> 00:01:35.494
Yeah, "You don't need to know
nothing about nothing."

00:01:35.561 --> 00:01:38.431
-You know nothing?
Come see our movie.

00:01:38.498 --> 00:01:41.133
-Uh, you play a paramedic
and a clairvoyant in this film.

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:43.135
-Yes.
-Would you enjoy having --

00:01:43.202 --> 00:01:45.070 line:91%
I mean, again,
I feel like when people

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:47.406
have clairvoyance in films,
that they always have

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:48.540
that thing happen,
where they see terrible...

00:01:48.607 --> 00:01:49.976
-Where they go
[Imitates explosion]

00:01:50.042 --> 00:01:51.844
-Yeah. And then it's always --
they always jump ahead

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:53.112
to some awful --
-Did you like that?

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:54.013
-That was really good.
-The sound effects?

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.447
-Did you do...

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:57.950
Now, you did your own
sound effects in the movie.

00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.618
-I did.
-Yeah.

00:01:59.685 --> 00:02:02.522
-[ Imitates whooshing ]
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.589 --> 00:02:04.290
-The Foley artists were like,
"You know what,

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:05.925
I think we should
just let her do it."

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:06.925
-"We can't do anything
without her."

00:02:06.992 --> 00:02:08.260
-"We can't beat it."

00:02:08.327 --> 00:02:09.729
That's the best noise
I've ever heard

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:11.998
for someone about to have
a clairvoyant moment.

00:02:12.065 --> 00:02:13.966
Would you like to have
the power of clairvoyance?

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:17.136
-No. I'm good.
-I think I'm good, too.

00:02:17.203 --> 00:02:20.006
-I feel like
I like being surprised.

00:02:20.073 --> 00:02:22.008
-Yeah.
-And I also --

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:24.577
It would be too much
responsibility.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:25.878
-You strike me as someone --

00:02:25.944 --> 00:02:28.247
I believe you like
being surprised,

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.216
but do you ever look surprised?

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:32.217
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.284 --> 00:02:36.488
Like, I would feel, if I was
at a surprise party for you

00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:37.923
and we all, like, jumped out,

00:02:37.990 --> 00:02:41.494
I feel like your expression
might not change at all.

00:02:41.561 --> 00:02:43.829
-Let's try. Get down.
-Okay, great.

00:02:43.896 --> 00:02:46.198
-Okay.
-Surprise!

00:02:46.265 --> 00:02:48.334
Yeah, that's it.

00:02:48.401 --> 00:02:50.002
That's exactly what I expected.

00:02:50.069 --> 00:02:52.672
[ Applause ]

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:54.073
I just had a surprise party.

00:02:54.139 --> 00:02:55.708
Same thing.
I do not surprise.

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.643
-You do not surprise?
-I do not surprise.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.246
I walked in and a bunch
of people yelled, "Surprise!"

00:03:00.313 --> 00:03:02.281
And my wife said
it was such a drag.

00:03:02.348 --> 00:03:03.816
She said,
"You have to try harder."

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:05.885
-What did you do?
-I went, "Oh, okay."

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.553
[ Laughter ]

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:09.556
'Cause then I was
doing the math of everything.

00:03:09.622 --> 00:03:11.491
Well, everything weird
that had happened

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:13.659
in the previous two hours.
I was like, "Okay."

00:03:13.725 --> 00:03:14.860
-Right then?
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:14.927 --> 00:03:16.228
-Not, like, later?
-And they were all like,

00:03:16.295 --> 00:03:19.198
"This is such a drag
as he's, like, doing the math."

00:03:19.265 --> 00:03:23.869
-Like, wait, "John, Susan.
My...

00:03:23.936 --> 00:03:26.639
Oh, and -- oh."
[ Laughs ]

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:29.642
-Well, this means
the world to me.

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:31.777
-Wow! [ Laughs ]

00:03:31.844 --> 00:03:34.546
-You are, uh,
you're a millennial.

00:03:34.613 --> 00:03:38.317
There are three other actors
in this film who are Gen Z,

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:41.887
and that kind of is one
of the relationships in the film

00:03:41.954 --> 00:03:44.490
is how a millennial
would react to Gen Z.

00:03:44.556 --> 00:03:47.159
How did you actually like
your Gen Z cast members,

00:03:47.226 --> 00:03:49.095
your co-stars?
-I love them.

00:03:49.162 --> 00:03:51.063
-Yeah.
-And they annoy me.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:52.765
[ Laughter ]

00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:55.468
-They annoy you
on a generational level?

00:03:55.534 --> 00:03:57.269
Like how they're
experiencing life?

00:03:57.336 --> 00:03:58.504
-Yes. [ Laughs ]
-Yeah.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.739
-What's something --
when do you feel --

00:04:00.806 --> 00:04:02.909
when did you feel the oldest?

00:04:02.976 --> 00:04:04.210
-All the time.
-Yeah.

00:04:04.277 --> 00:04:06.746
-Like, every second of the day.

00:04:06.812 --> 00:04:08.380
-That's awful. I can't...
-I know.

00:04:08.447 --> 00:04:10.149
-It breaks my heart because --

00:04:10.215 --> 00:04:12.184
-I'm not that much older
than them.

00:04:12.251 --> 00:04:14.187
-But it just -- I think
it's happening so fast now.

00:04:14.253 --> 00:04:15.654
These generations
are like turning over.

00:04:15.721 --> 00:04:18.323
-[ Imitates Seth ]
It's happening so fast.

00:04:18.390 --> 00:04:21.293
-Every day they got a new app!
-They get older and older.

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:24.831
Every day I get older.
It's crazy.

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:26.866
-Hey, we we were co-stars
in something,

00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:29.268
which I did not realize
until today.

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:32.338
You were in the final episode
of "The Office."

00:04:32.405 --> 00:04:34.507
You were! This is --
and it's kind of crazy because,

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:37.543
like, this is a super -- I mean,
obviously a seminal show.

00:04:37.609 --> 00:04:39.412
And you were only
in the final episode.

00:04:39.478 --> 00:04:42.214
-I was in the series finale
of "The Office."

00:04:42.281 --> 00:04:43.582
You were in that?

00:04:43.649 --> 00:04:47.720
-I was in it because
there was a clip where we --

00:04:47.787 --> 00:04:50.056
this was in the final episode
of "The Office,"

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:52.157
that there was
a clip of "Weekend Update"

00:04:52.224 --> 00:04:54.560
making fun of, um,
Ed Helms' character.

00:04:54.626 --> 00:04:59.098
-God, that was honestly
the worst time of my life.

00:04:59.165 --> 00:05:00.299
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:03.569
I loved that show so much.
-Of course.

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:05.872
-And they were like,

00:05:05.938 --> 00:05:07.273
"Do you want to be
in the series finale?"

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:08.608
And I was like, "Of course,"

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:11.243
thinking that I'd show up
for like half a day,

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.512
and I was there for two weeks,

00:05:13.579 --> 00:05:16.048
and I'm barely
in the [bleep] show.

00:05:16.115 --> 00:05:17.616
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:17.683 --> 00:05:20.820
I would also imagine --
wasn't everybody super sad?

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:22.155
-They were sad.

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:24.289
And they also, like, there
were weird dynamics

00:05:24.356 --> 00:05:26.458
that had been going on
for the last 10 years.

00:05:26.525 --> 00:05:27.960
Some people didn't speak
to each other,

00:05:28.027 --> 00:05:31.297
and I'm coming in like [Laughs]
"I'm so excited to be here!"

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:32.999
And no one wanted to talk to me.

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:35.067
Nobody gave a [bleep]
and I like --

00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:36.102
sorry I'm swearing so much.

00:05:36.168 --> 00:05:37.337
I don't --
-No.

00:05:37.403 --> 00:05:38.771
-It's 'cause I had a glass
of champagne.

00:05:38.837 --> 00:05:40.205
-You know, look, it's
"The [bleep] Office's" fault.

00:05:40.272 --> 00:05:41.307
It's not your fault.

00:05:41.374 --> 00:05:42.842
-It's their fault.

00:05:42.909 --> 00:05:44.043
And I was, like,

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.512
in the background
of all of these scenes.

00:05:46.579 --> 00:05:47.780
-Oh, right.
That's the whole thing.

00:05:47.846 --> 00:05:49.982
-Faxing things,
and I don't know.

00:05:50.049 --> 00:05:53.386
-Can I tell you,
I recently watched it

00:05:53.452 --> 00:05:55.855
and it was some of the most
believable faxing

00:05:55.922 --> 00:05:57.523
I've ever seen.

00:05:57.589 --> 00:05:59.358
And a lot of people,
I think, mail it in

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:01.026
when they're in the background
of those shows.

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:02.261
But I was just like,
"Oh, my God!"

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:03.529
It was crazy.

00:06:03.596 --> 00:06:06.699
-I was like, well, if I'm here,
then I'm here.

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:08.467
I'm gonna deliver.

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.302
-Please come back soon.

00:06:10.369 --> 00:06:11.436
It has been too long.
-No.

00:06:11.503 --> 00:06:13.038
-I know. I know you won't,

00:06:13.105 --> 00:06:14.273
but it's fun.
-I will.

00:06:14.339 --> 00:06:15.774
I actually have a little bit
of clairvoyance,

00:06:15.841 --> 00:06:17.843
and you're coming back.
-Okay.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.379
-You guys,
that's Dakota Johnson.

00:06:20.446 --> 00:06:23.215
-"Madame Web," only in theaters
and IMAX on February 14th.

00:06:23.282 --> 00:06:26.119
We'll be right back
with Sheryl Lee Ralph.

